Practical 5: Understanding How High-Level C Language Constructs are Translated into Assembly Language
Aim

To understand how high-level C language constructs are translated into assembly language instructions.

Objectives
To write a simple C program
To compile the program using GCC
To generate assembly code from C source code
To analyze how constructs like variables, arithmetic operations, and conditional statements are translated into assembly
Procedure (Step-by-Step on Linux)
Step 1: Create a C program
nano test.c

Add the following code:

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    int b = 3;
    int c = a + b;

    if (c > 5) {
        printf("Greater\n");
    } else {
        printf("Smaller\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
Step 2: Compile the program
gcc test.c -o test

Run the program:

./test
Step 3: Generate assembly code
gcc -S test.c

This generates an assembly file named test.s.

Step 4: View assembly code
cat test.s
Step 5: Analyze the translation
Variables (a, b, c) are stored in registers or memory
Arithmetic operation (a + b) is translated into add instruction
Conditional statement (if-else) is translated into comparison (cmp) and jump instructions (jg, jl, etc.)
Function call (printf) is represented using call instruction
Outcome

The C program was successfully compiled and converted into assembly code, demonstrating how high-level constructs are translated into low-level instructions.

Result

The assembly output showed how variables, arithmetic operations, and conditional statements in C are represented using low-level instructions such as mov, add, cmp, and call.
